  "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/a22dba264faa711352724f135d2ec5a074202c4b5069dee2389fe954419a3770.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$",
